        <body>

<h2>Requesting a Special Education Evaluation in District 16: A Parent's Guide</h2>

<h3>Local Context: Navigating Special Education in Bedford-Stuyvesant</h3>

<p>If you reside in Bedford-Stuyvesant, the heart of District 16, and have concerns about your child's learning, behavior, or development, you have a legal right to request a special education evaluation. District 16, like many urban districts, faces unique challenges. It's statistically important to note that District 16 can have a higher referral rate to District 75 (D75) specialized programs for students with more significant needs, compared to some other districts in NYC. Before pursuing a special education evaluation, we strongly encourage you to explore all available options for support within your child's zoned school and document these attempts meticulously. Remember, under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA), the least restrictive environment (LRE) is paramount (34 CFR § 300.114).</p>

<p>District 16 schools are supposed to provide behavioral supports and related services, such as speech therapy, occupational therapy, and counseling, within the general education setting whenever appropriate and feasible. This aligns with the principles of Response to Intervention (RTI), a multi-tiered approach designed to identify and support struggling learners early on. (See NYS Part 200.1(ww) for the definition of RTI.) Before formally requesting an evaluation, schedule meetings with your child's teacher, the school psychologist, and the guidance counselor. Specifically inquire about: </p>
<ul>
    <li><b>Available academic interventions</b> (e.g., small group tutoring, reading support, math remediation).</li>
    <li><b>Behavioral support plans</b> (e.g., positive behavior interventions and supports (PBIS), behavior charts, individual counseling).</li>
    <li><b>Related services</b> (e.g., speech therapy screening, occupational therapy consultation).</li>
</ul>
<p>Many behavior challenges and academic difficulties can be effectively addressed through proactive classroom interventions and targeted supports. Document all interventions attempted, their duration, and their impact on your child. This documentation will be valuable, regardless of whether you ultimately pursue a special education evaluation.</p>

<h3>The Written Referral: Your Starting Point</h3>

<p>The formal special education process begins with a <b>written</b> referral to the Committee on Special Education (CSE). This referral is a crucial document, and it should be drafted with care. The referral must clearly and specifically state your concerns about your child's academic, behavioral, or developmental progress. Vague statements are not enough. Provide concrete examples of the challenges your child is experiencing, and support your concerns with any relevant documentation you possess (e.g., doctor's reports, private evaluations, report cards, previous school records, observations you have made at home).</p>

<p>Address your written request directly to the CSE Chairperson for District 16. Obtain the current contact information for the Chairperson from your child's school. Include your child's full name, date of birth, address, and the name of the school they attend. Be sure to keep a copy of the referral for your records, and send it via certified mail with return receipt requested to have proof that the district received it.</p>

<p>Your referral letter should include, but not be limited to, the following elements:</p>
<ol>
    <li><b>A clear statement requesting a special education evaluation</b>. For example: "I am writing to formally request a special education evaluation for my child, [Child's Full Name], date of birth [Date of Birth], who attends [School Name] in District 16."</li>
    <li><b>A detailed description of your concerns</b>. Provide specific examples. For instance: "My child is significantly behind in reading. They are unable to decode simple words and struggle with reading comprehension. According to their report card, they are performing below grade level in ELA." Or, "My child exhibits frequent outbursts in class and has difficulty following directions. They have been suspended twice for aggressive behavior towards other students."</li>
    <li><b>A summary of any interventions attempted and their results</b>. For example: "The teacher implemented a reading intervention program for six weeks, but my child's reading skills did not improve significantly. I have attached documentation from the teacher outlining the intervention plan."</li>
    <li><b>Copies of relevant documentation</b>. Include any doctor's reports, previous evaluations, report cards, or other documents that support your concerns.</li>
    <li><b>Your contact information</b>, including your phone number and email address.</li>
</ol>

<p><b>Sample Referral Letter Excerpt:</b></p>
<p style="font-style: italic;">"Dear CSE Chairperson,</p>

<p style="font-style: italic;">I am writing to request a special education evaluation for my daughter, [Child's Name], born on [Date of Birth]. She is currently in [Grade] at [School Name]. I am deeply concerned about her academic progress in mathematics. She consistently scores poorly on math tests and struggles to grasp basic concepts such as fractions and decimals. Despite extra help from her teacher after school, her performance has not improved. I have attached her latest report card, which shows a failing grade in mathematics, as well as samples of her classwork demonstrating significant difficulty with problem-solving. I believe a comprehensive evaluation is necessary to determine if she has a learning disability and to identify appropriate interventions and support services. Thank you for your time and attention to this important matter."</p>

<ul>
  <li><b>Be specific about your concerns.</b> Avoid vague language.</li>
  <li><b>Include all relevant documentation.</b> The more information you provide, the better.</li>
  <li><b>Address the request to the CSE Chairperson, District 16.</b> Make sure you have the correct name and contact information.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The 60-Day Timeline: Your Rights and Responsibilities</h3>

<p>Once the CSE receives your written referral, a legally mandated 60-day timeline (excluding summer breaks) begins (NYS Part 200.4(a)). This timeline is critical, and it's your responsibility to monitor it closely. Within these 60 days, the CSE must:</p>

<ol>
    <li><b>Review your referral and determine if an evaluation is warranted.</b> In rare instances, the CSE may determine that an evaluation is not necessary. If this happens, they must provide you with written notification explaining their reasoning and informing you of your right to appeal this decision (NYS Part 200.5(a)).</li>
    <li><b>Obtain parental consent for the evaluation.</b> The CSE must obtain your written consent before proceeding with any assessments (34 CFR § 300.300).</li>
    <li><b>Conduct all necessary assessments.</b> This may include psychological evaluations, educational assessments, speech and language evaluations, occupational therapy evaluations, and other assessments as needed to fully understand your child's strengths and weaknesses. Ensure that all assessments are conducted in your child's native language or mode of communication (34 CFR § 300.304).</li>
    <li><b>Hold a CSE meeting.</b> Once the evaluations are complete, the CSE will schedule a meeting to discuss the results and determine your child's eligibility for special education services. You, as the parent, are a vital member of the CSE team and have the right to participate fully in all discussions and decisions (34 CFR § 300.322).</li>
    <li><b>Provide you with a written determination of eligibility.</b> After the CSE meeting, you will receive a written copy of the Individualized Education Program (IEP) if your child is found eligible or a written explanation if your child is found ineligible.</li>
</ol>

<p><b>Important Considerations Regarding the 60-Day Timeline:</b></p>

<ul>
    <li><b>Summer Breaks:</b> The 60-day timeline excludes the period from July 1st through August 31st. If you submit a referral in June, the timeline will be paused during the summer and resume on September 1st.</li>
    <li><b>Parental Delays:</b> If you cause significant delays in the evaluation process (e.g., by repeatedly rescheduling appointments or failing to provide necessary information), the CSE may argue that the 60-day timeline should be extended. However, minor delays should not significantly impact the timeline.</li>
    <li><b>District Delays:</b> The district is responsible for ensuring that the evaluation process is completed within the 60-day timeline. If the district fails to meet this deadline, you have the right to file a complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED) (NYS Part 200.5(l)).</li>
    <li><b>Document Everything:</b> Keep a detailed record of all communication with the CSE, including dates, times, and the names of the individuals you spoke with. This documentation will be invaluable if you need to advocate for your child's rights.</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Proactive Follow-Up is Key:</b> Do not passively wait for the CSE to contact you. Proactively follow up with the CSE Chairperson to inquire about the status of the evaluation. If you haven't heard back within two weeks of submitting your referral, send a follow-up email or letter. Continue to follow up regularly throughout the 60-day timeline. Document every interaction you have with the CSE, including the date, time, and the name of the person you spoke with.</p>

<h3>Common Pitfalls and How to Avoid Them</h3>

<p>Navigating the special education evaluation process in District 16 (or any NYC district) can be challenging. Here are some common pitfalls parents encounter and strategies to avoid them:</p>

<ol>
    <li><b>Vague Referrals:</b> As mentioned earlier, a vague referral will likely result in delays or an inadequate evaluation. Be specific and provide concrete examples of your concerns.</li>
    <li><b>Incomplete Documentation:</b> Ensure you include all relevant documentation with your referral. This includes doctor's reports, previous evaluations, report cards, and any other information that supports your concerns.</li>
    <li><b>Passive Waiting:</b> Don't simply submit your referral and wait for the CSE to contact you. Proactively follow up and stay engaged in the process.</li>
    <li><b>Intimidation by the CSE:</b> Remember that you are an equal member of the CSE team and have the right to advocate for your child's needs. Don't be afraid to ask questions, express your concerns, and challenge the CSE's recommendations if you disagree with them.</li>
    <li><b>Accepting the CSE's Recommendations Without Question:</b> The CSE's recommendations are not necessarily set in stone. You have the right to obtain an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) at public expense if you disagree with the CSE's evaluation (34 CFR § 300.502). You also have the right to request mediation or file a complaint with NYSED if you are unable to resolve your disagreements with the CSE.</li>
    <li><b>Lack of Understanding of Your Rights:</b> Familiarize yourself with your rights under IDEA and New York State special education law. The more you know, the better equipped you will be to advocate for your child.</li>
    <li><b>Failing to Document Everything:</b> Keep a detailed record of all communication with the CSE, including dates, times, and the names of the individuals you spoke with. This documentation will be invaluable if you need to advocate for your child's rights.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h3>

<dl>
  <dt><b>Q: What if the CSE denies my request for an evaluation?</b></dt>
  <dd>A: The CSE must provide you with a written explanation of their reasons for denying your request. You have the right to appeal this decision by requesting mediation or filing a complaint with NYSED.</dd>

  <dt><b>Q: What if I disagree with the results of the CSE's evaluation?</b></dt>
  <dd>A: You have the right to obtain an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) at public expense if you disagree with the CSE's evaluation. You must request this IEE in writing, and the CSE must either grant your request or initiate a due process hearing to demonstrate that their evaluation was appropriate.</dd>

  <dt><b>Q: What if the CSE does not meet the 60-day timeline?</b></dt>
  <dd>A: If the CSE fails to meet the 60-day timeline, you have the right to file a complaint with NYSED. You may also be able to request compensatory services for your child to make up for the delays.</dd>

  <dt><b>Q: Can I bring an advocate or attorney to the CSE meeting?</b></dt>
  <dd>A: Yes, you have the right to bring an advocate or attorney to any CSE meeting. An advocate or attorney can help you understand your rights, navigate the special education process, and advocate for your child's needs.</dd>

  <dt><b>Q: What is an IEP?</b></dt>
  <dd>A: An IEP, or Individualized Education Program, is a written plan that outlines the special education and related services that your child will receive. The IEP is developed by the CSE and must be tailored to meet your child's unique needs. The IEP includes goals, accommodations, and modifications that will help your child succeed in school.</dd>
</dl>

</body>
